<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/docs/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/docs/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/docs/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/docs/Section.abide'
import Library from './Library.abide'
import Shelves from './Shelves.abide'
import Volume from './Volume.abide'
import librarySource from './Library.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import nowPlayingSource from './NowPlaying.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import queueControlSource from './QueueControl.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import shelfSource from './Shelf.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import shelvesSource from './Shelves.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import trackRowSource from './TrackRow.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import volumeSource from './Volume.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <p>Where a value really is one thing for the whole app, <strong>reach for a module
    first</strong>: export the value, import it wherever you read it, and you have it typed, with no
    key, no registry and no lifetime of its own to reason about. <code>reactive.share</code> is for
    the value that is one thing per <em>subtree</em> — the case a module cannot reach, because two
    shelves each want their own.</p>

    <Section title="The forms of `reactive.share`">
        <p><code>reactive.share</code> is get-or-create in the current scope, by key. One call, and
        the key is a literal.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What you get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>reactive.share(key, build)</code></td><td>the value under <code>key</code>, built by <code>build</code> only if nobody above you had one</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive.share(key, build)</code> again</td><td>the same value, and this call's <code>build</code> never runs</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>reactive.share(expression, build)</code></td><td>a build error — the key is a literal or it is nothing</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>What comes back is whatever the thunk built, which is an ordinary <code>Reactive</code>
        with no share-shaped wrapper on it. <code>Shared</code> is the optional registry of key
        names, and both are in the <a href={url('/reference/reactive')}><code>reactive</code>
        reference</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Two components far apart read one value">
        <Example
            id="share-agree"
            title="`reactive.share(key, build)` — get-or-create in this scope"
            summary="A now-playing bar over a library of tracks. Press a title and watch the bar, which the library never speaks to."
            files={{
                'Library.abide': librarySource,
                'NowPlaying.abide': nowPlayingSource,
                'TrackRow.abide': trackRowSource,
            }}
        >
            <Library />
        </Example>

        <p><em>3 instances, 1 value, 0 props</em> — against a prop through the list, or a store
        module with a lifetime of its own.</p>

        <p>The page holds the key, so every instance below it finds that one on the way up and none
        of their <code>build</code> thunks run — three rows and a bar, one value. The page declares
        it and hands neither of them anything.</p>

        <p>That is the case a prop cannot reach. Where a parent already has the value, hand it down
        — a plain <code>T</code> prop is a <strong>live read</strong> of the caller's expression, so
        <code>{'title={track}'}</code> follows <code>track</code> and the child re-renders without
        being reinstantiated. Reach for <code>reactive.share</code> when the path between the
        readers is uninterested, and for a prop when the parent that owns the value is the one
        rendering the reader.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/templates/reuse-a-piece-of-markup')}>Components</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/bind-a-form-to-state')}>Form binding</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`reactive.share` hands back the one that won">
        <Example
            id="share-winner"
            title="`reactive.share` hands back the one that won"
            summary="One key asked for twice, once properly and once with the value built outside the call. Step the volume and watch the third tile."
            files={{
                'Volume.abide': volumeSource,
            }}
        >
            <Volume />
        </Example>

        <p>One call rather than a share-then-read pair, so there is no ordering hazard and no miss
        to define. So <strong>use the return value and no other name.</strong></p>

        <p>Building the value outside the call and passing it in fails silently, which is what the
        third tile is. The local name goes on being read by the module that made it while every
        other module reads the shared one, and both are correct-looking — they even agree until
        somebody writes. Handing a thunk instead keeps the loser's value from being built at
        all.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`Shared` is your app's own registry of keys">
        <p><code>Shared</code> is your app's own registry, widened by declaration merging — and an
        <strong>opt-in tightening</strong>, not a precondition. A key it declares is checked against
        it; a key it does not is inferred from the thunk, so <code>reactive.share</code> works
        before you have written one.</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="src/shared.ts — excerpt" code={`declare module '@abide/abide' {
    interface Shared {
        nowPlaying: Reactive<string>
    }
}`} />

        <p>The registry buys one thing: two modules cannot disagree about the value under one name.
        With the key declared, a typo is a compile error and the value type follows from the key.
        Without one, <code>reactive.share('scratch', () =&gt; reactive(0))</code> is a
        <code>Reactive&lt;number&gt;</code> inferred from what you built.</p>

        <p>A <strong>dynamic key is a build error</strong>, declared or not, and that is deliberate
        rather than a limitation to work around: what a dynamic key would reach for is a keyed
        value, holding one entry per args key. It also keeps the set of shared keys fixed by the
        source, so there is no runtime bound to enforce and nothing to refuse.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/let-anything-publish-and-anything-read')}>Channels</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A shared value lives in the component that created it, and below">
        <Example
            id="share-scope"
            title="A shared value lives where it was created, and below"
            summary="Two shelves rendered from one component, each with a control below it asking for the same key. Queue on one shelf and watch the other."
            files={{
                'Shelves.abide': shelvesSource,
                'Shelf.abide': shelfSource,
                'QueueControl.abide': queueControlSource,
            }}
        >
            <Shelves />
        </Example>

        <p>The scope is <strong>the component instance and its descendants</strong>. A key found on
        the way up is the one you get; a key nothing above you holds is created where you asked for
        it. So each shelf creates its own <code>queued</code>, the control below it finds that one
        rather than building a second, and neither shelf can see the other's.</p>

        <p>That scoping is what makes a short word safe as a key. A registry keyed process-wide
        would make <code>queued</code> a name two unrelated parts of an app collide on in silence,
        and neither side could see the collision.</p>

        <p>So the scope is the choice. <strong>One per subtree is <code>reactive.share</code>; one
        for the app is a module</strong>, and reaching for a key where a module would have done buys
        a registry, a literal to keep in step and a scope to think about, in exchange for
        nothing.</p>

        <p>A shared value is <strong>never evicted within its scope</strong> (10.4). It goes when
        the scope does and not before, because dropping one would orphan live readers that go on
        reading something nobody can refresh. Even a <code>global</code> entry, which can be
        rebuilt, is bounded only by its own <code>ttl</code> and by <code>invalidate</code> (11.35,
        11.36).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/load-once-per-set-of-arguments')}>Caching</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/data/keep-a-room-of-callers-in-sync')}>Live updates</a> — sharing
            across callers rather than components</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/load-once-per-set-of-arguments')}>Caching</a> —
            <code>global</code>, and why its rule runs the other way</li>
            <li><a href={url('/values/do-something-when-a-value-changes')}>Watching values</a> —
            reacting to a shared value from anywhere</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
